{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_86","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"caption\":\"United States v. Oliveras\",\"court\":\"2d Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-03-15\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 21-2954\",\"proposition\":\"The special-needs doctrine can support a supervised-release condition authorizing suspicionless probation-officer searches, but the condition requires an individualized record showing and may impose no greater liberty deprivation than reasonably necessary.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"special needs / supervised release\",\"discovery_path\":\"special-needs sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Hay\",\"court\":\"10th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-03-19\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"95 F.4th 1304\",\"proposition\":\"Long-term pole-camera observation of a home's publicly visible exterior was not a Fourth Amendment search; the court declined to extend Carpenter's mosaic reasoning beyond the categories Carpenter addressed.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Katz / public-view surveillance / mosaic theory\",\"discovery_path\":\"pole-camera circuit split → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Johnson v. Smith\",\"court\":\"10th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-06-10\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-3091\",\"proposition\":\"Licensing a home-based business does not itself constitute blanket consent to warrantless inspection; a regulatory search requires a closely regulated industry and satisfaction of the Burger necessity and warrant-substitute safeguards.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"administrative searches / closely regulated industries\",\"discovery_path\":\"special-needs and administrative-search sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Chiaverini v. City of Napoleon, Ohio\",\"court\":\"U.S.\",\"date\":\"2024-06-20\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"602 U.S. 556; No. 23-50\",\"proposition\":\"Probable cause for one charge does not categorically defeat a Fourth Amendment malicious-prosecution claim based on another baseless charge that caused or prolonged a pretrial seizure.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"seizure through legal process / charge-specific probable cause\",\"discovery_path\":\"SCOTUS merits sweep → official opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Chatrie\",\"court\":\"4th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-07-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"107 F.4th 319; No. 22-4489\",\"proposition\":\"The panel held that obtaining two hours of opted-in Google Location History through a geofence warrant was not a search because it did not reveal the whole of the defendant's movements and the data had been voluntarily exposed to Google; vacated upon rehearing en banc.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Katz / geofence data / third-party doctrine\",\"discovery_path\":\"geofence-warrant split → published panel opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Cuevas v. City of Tulare\",\"court\":\"9th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-07-10\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"107 F.4th 894; No. 23-15953\",\"proposition\":\"A fleeing driver's car becoming stuck in mud was not a seizure because the terminating force was not intentionally applied by government, while a police-dog bite and officers' gunfire were force applied with objective intent to restrain.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Torres line / means intentionally applied\",\"discovery_path\":\"Torres protest-and-force citation chain → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Marks v. Bauer\",\"court\":\"8th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-07-12\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"107 F.4th 842; No. 23-1420\",\"proposition\":\"An officer seized a protester by deliberately aiming and firing a chemical projectile that stopped him; the officer's testimony that he used force to restrain movement distinguished targeted force from general crowd dispersal.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Torres line / targeted less-lethal projectile\",\"discovery_path\":\"Torres crowd-control citation chain → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Davis\",\"court\":\"11th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-07-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"109 F.4th 1320; No. 23-10184\",\"proposition\":\"A defendant lacked Fourth Amendment standing to challenge geofence data associated with another person's device and account; the limited public movements disclosed did not establish invasion of his own protected privacy interest.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"whose rights / geofence standing\",\"discovery_path\":\"geofence-warrant split → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Smith\",\"court\":\"5th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-08-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"110 F.4th 817; No. 23-60321\",\"proposition\":\"Compelling Google to search its entire location-history repository for devices within a geofence was governmental action and invaded users' reasonable expectations of privacy; the warrants were unconstitutional general warrants, although good faith prevented suppression.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Katz / geofence data / general warrants\",\"discovery_path\":\"geofence-warrant split → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Asinor v. District of Columbia\",\"court\":\"D.C. Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-08-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"Nos. 22-7129, 22-7130\",\"proposition\":\"A lawful seizure of effects can become unreasonable when government continues retaining them after its legitimate purpose has ended; text, founding-era dictionaries, Blackstone, common-law property remedies, and modern duration precedents establish that a seizure remains subject to reasonableness throughout its duration.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"property seizure / duration / text and history\",\"discovery_path\":\"property-seizure and text-history sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Sanderlin v. Dwyer\",\"court\":\"9th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-09-04\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"116 F.4th 905; No. 23-15487\",\"proposition\":\"Firing a foam-baton round that incapacitated a protester could be a seizure because the weapon and manner of use objectively manifested intent to restrain; an officer's asserted subjective purpose to disperse rather than apprehend was not controlling.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Torres line / objective intent to restrain\",\"discovery_path\":\"Torres crowd-control citation chain → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Maher\",\"court\":\"2d Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-10-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"120 F.4th 297; No. 23-6181\",\"proposition\":\"A provider's automated hash match did not frustrate the user's privacy interest in an image's contents; police performed a new search when they visually opened the file, although good faith prevented suppression.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"private-search doctrine / automated hash matching\",\"discovery_path\":\"private-search and NCMEC sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Chatrie\",\"court\":\"4th Cir. en banc\",\"date\":\"2024-11-01\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 22-4489\",\"proposition\":\"The court granted rehearing en banc and vacated the panel's precedential geofence ruling, setting up the circuit's deeply fractured reconsideration of privacy, third-party doctrine, and general-warrant principles.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"geofence data / major en banc grant\",\"discovery_path\":\"panel follow-up → en banc grant order\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. House\",\"court\":\"7th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-11-05\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"120 F.4th 1313; No. 23-1950\",\"proposition\":\"Pole-camera recording of activities exposed to public view outside a residence was not a search; aggregation and duration did not create a Carpenter-style mosaic claim on the facts presented.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Katz / public-view surveillance / mosaic theory\",\"discovery_path\":\"pole-camera circuit sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Holmes\",\"court\":\"9th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-11-13\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"121 F.4th 727; No. 22-10266\",\"proposition\":\"Federal agents conducted a search when they opened and viewed Facebook files that neither the provider nor NCMEC had previously viewed; the private-search doctrine, good faith, and inevitable discovery did not justify the warrantless inspection.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"private-search doctrine / NCMEC referrals\",\"discovery_path\":\"private-search and NCMEC sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Puente v. City of Phoenix\",\"court\":\"9th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-12-19\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"123 F.4th 1035; No. 22-15344\",\"proposition\":\"Chemical agents deployed to disperse and exclude a crowd did not effect seizures where the officers' conduct objectively manifested an intent to make people leave, not to detain or otherwise restrain them.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Torres line / dispersal versus restraint\",\"discovery_path\":\"Torres crowd-control citation chain → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Gregory\",\"court\":\"11th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-02-13\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"128 F.4th 1228; Nos. 22-12800, 22-12809, 22-12843\",\"proposition\":\"Extended pole-camera surveillance of areas outside a home that were exposed to public observation was not a search; the court rejected a Carpenter-based aggregation theory.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Katz / public-view surveillance / mosaic theory\",\"discovery_path\":\"pole-camera circuit sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Harry\",\"court\":\"2d Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-03-07\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"130 F.4th 342; No. 23-7106\",\"proposition\":\"Pole-camera surveillance of a home's publicly exposed exterior was not a search, and aggregating lawful observations did not convert them into a Carpenter-style mosaic search.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Katz / public-view surveillance / mosaic theory\",\"discovery_path\":\"pole-camera circuit sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Peck\",\"court\":\"8th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-03-12\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"131 F.4th 629; No. 24-1198\",\"proposition\":\"Under circuit precedent, a drug-dog sniff at an apartment door was not a search because it revealed only contraband; a concurrence maintained that the intrusion should count as a search under modern home-and-curtilage doctrine.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Jones / apartment-door dog sniff / curtilage\",\"discovery_path\":\"dog-sniff and trespass split → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Robinson\",\"court\":\"2d Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-04-14\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-8022\",\"proposition\":\"A supervised-release electronic-search condition was evaluated under special needs by considering the supervision purpose, diminished privacy and notice, and whether the intrusion bore a close and substantial relationship to the supervisory need.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"special needs / electronic searches of supervisees\",\"discovery_path\":\"special-needs sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Chatrie\",\"court\":\"4th Cir. en banc\",\"date\":\"2025-04-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"136 F.4th 100; No. 22-4489\",\"proposition\":\"The en banc court affirmed denial of suppression but produced no precedential majority on whether the geofence acquisition was a search: seven judges said yes, seven said no, and one avoided the issue, with good faith supplying the controlling disposition.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Katz / geofence data / fractured en banc decision\",\"discovery_path\":\"en banc grant follow-up → published en banc opinions\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"The Heidi Group, Inc. v. Texas Health & Human Services Commission\",\"court\":\"5th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-05-28\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-50303\",\"proposition\":\"Cloud-stored business documents retained Fourth Amendment protection despite third-party storage and provider terms; a former employee became a government agent when a state investigator encouraged and directed her Dropbox search.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"government actor / cloud-data privacy / third-party storage\",\"discovery_path\":\"government-agent and cloud-search sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Hines\",\"court\":\"2d Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-06-11\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"140 F.4th 105; No. 23-7032\",\"proposition\":\"The defendant bore the burden of showing governmental participation in a private phone search; a girlfriend who independently unlocked and scrolled the phone was not a police agent, and an officer's mere visual observation of images she displayed was not a search.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"government actor / private phone search\",\"discovery_path\":\"private-search sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Kanuszewski v. Michigan Department of Health & Human Services\",\"court\":\"6th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-06-25\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-1733\",\"proposition\":\"Only information-seeking use of retained newborn blood spots constitutes a search; storage and non-informational handling alone do not. Continued retention can be a seizure only if plaintiffs establish a protected possessory interest, which they failed to do under governing state law.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"definition of search / biological samples / property seizure\",\"discovery_path\":\"search-definition and retained-property sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"McMurry v. Weaver\",\"court\":\"5th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-06-27\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-50571\",\"proposition\":\"A child-welfare home search was not justified by special needs or community caretaking when intertwined with ordinary law enforcement; absent consent or exigency, entry ordinarily requires judicial authorization, and child removal requires an order, consent, or immediate-danger exigency.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"special needs / child-welfare investigations / home entry\",\"discovery_path\":\"special-needs and child-welfare sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Cheairs v. City of Seattle\",\"court\":\"9th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-08-01\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"145 F.4th 1233; No. 24-3163\",\"proposition\":\"Evidence that an officer threw a blast ball overhand between protesters and a police line created a triable dispute over whether the conduct objectively manifested intent to restrain rather than merely disperse.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Torres line / objective intent / crowd-control munition\",\"discovery_path\":\"Torres crowd-control citation chain → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Johnson\",\"court\":\"4th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-08-05\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"148 F.4th 287; No. 23-4255\",\"proposition\":\"A warrantless drug-dog sniff at an apartment door in a locked common hallway was not a search: the contraband-only sniff doctrine applied, and the shared hallway was not curtilage because the tenant lacked a right to exclude others.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Jones and Katz / apartment-door dog sniff / curtilage\",\"discovery_path\":\"dog-sniff and trespass split → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Green\",\"court\":\"D.C. Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-08-12\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-3100\",\"proposition\":\"Brief pole-camera surveillance of a shared, publicly exposed courtyard was not a search; the public-view rule controlled, curtilage did not independently matter absent physical trespass, and the short monitoring period did not establish a mosaic search.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Katz and Jones / pole camera / curtilage\",\"discovery_path\":\"pole-camera circuit sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Guard\",\"court\":\"2d Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-09-10\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-6886\",\"proposition\":\"NCMEC is a governmental entity for Fourth Amendment purposes, but Kik's PhotoDNA scan was not attributable to government merely because it used NCMEC-supplied hashes; government compulsion, encouragement, or a sufficiently close nexus remained necessary.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"government actor / NCMEC / automated provider scanning\",\"discovery_path\":\"private-search and NCMEC sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Hawatmeh v. City of Henderson\",\"court\":\"9th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-11-07\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"159 F.4th 591; No. 24-6146\",\"proposition\":\"Police did not seize a hostage they unintentionally shot during a rescue attempt because their force did not objectively manifest an intent to restrain the hostage; intent directed at the captor could not be transferred for Fourth Amendment purposes.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Torres line / accidental target / intent to restrain\",\"discovery_path\":\"Torres force-intent citation chain → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Keup v. Palmer\",\"court\":\"8th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-11-19\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-1114\",\"proposition\":\"A pepper ball fired to disperse a protester did not objectively manifest intent to restrain, and officers' later movement of the injured protester to facilitate medical care likewise was not a seizure.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Torres line / dispersal and medical assistance\",\"discovery_path\":\"Torres crowd-control citation chain → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Case v. Montana\",\"court\":\"U.S.\",\"date\":\"2026-01-14\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"146 S. Ct. 500; No. 24-624\",\"proposition\":\"The emergency-aid exception permits warrantless home entry when officers have an objectively reasonable basis to believe someone inside needs emergency assistance; the Fourth Amendment does not require probable cause of an emergency.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"purpose-based reasonableness / emergency aid / home search\",\"discovery_path\":\"SCOTUS merits sweep → official opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Fellmy\",\"court\":\"6th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2026-01-23\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 25-5381\",\"proposition\":\"A drug dog's jumping onto and touching a vehicle's exterior was not a Jones search because founding-era trespass to chattels required dispossession, impairment, or damage, none of which the contact caused.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Jones trespass revival / effects / trespass to chattels\",\"discovery_path\":\"trespassory-search sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Lowers\",\"court\":\"4th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2026-03-10\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-4546\",\"proposition\":\"Files in a cloud account retained Fourth Amendment protection despite provider terms; a hash-value flag did not expose file contents, so an officer conducted a new search by opening the file, although attenuation prevented suppression.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"private-search doctrine / cloud files / hash matching\",\"discovery_path\":\"private-search and cloud-data sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Porter\",\"court\":\"5th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2026-03-17\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 25-60163\",\"proposition\":\"Querying a sparse local license-plate-reader network was not a search where ten cameras captured intermittent travel on public roads; the limited record resembled Knotts rather than comprehensive CSLI or geofence surveillance.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Katz / automated license-plate readers / mosaic theory\",\"discovery_path\":\"location-surveillance sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"O.W. v. Carr\",\"court\":\"4th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2026-04-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-1288\",\"proposition\":\"T.L.O.'s reasonable-suspicion framework remains controlling for a school administrator's cellphone search notwithstanding Riley; an SRO's presence and later receipt of the evidence did not transform the administrator's search into a law-enforcement search.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"school searches / government purpose / T.L.O. after Riley\",\"discovery_path\":\"special-needs and school-search sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Epps v. Christian\",\"court\":\"10th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2026-04-21\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-1371\",\"proposition\":\"An officer seized a protester by intentionally striking her with a pepper ball to exercise physical control; under Torres, continued movement after impact does not negate a seizure accomplished by force with objective intent to restrain.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Torres line / pepper-ball seizure\",\"discovery_path\":\"Torres crowd-control citation chain → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Chatrie v. United States\",\"court\":\"U.S.\",\"date\":\"2026-06-29\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 25-112; 2026 U.S. LEXIS 2878\",\"proposition\":\"Government acquisition of Google Location History through the challenged geofence process was a search: even short-term location data can invade a reasonable expectation of privacy, and ordinary cellphone use did not amount to voluntary exposure under the third-party doctrine. The Court remanded for stage-specific probable-cause and particularity analysis.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Katz / geofence data / third-party doctrine\",\"discovery_path\":\"SCOTUS geofence docket → official merits opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Brillhart\",\"court\":\"11th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2026-07-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-13226\",\"proposition\":\"After a private FedEx employee opened a package, agents could replicate and verify that search without a warrant so long as their more detailed inspection revealed nothing beyond the private search's scope.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"private-search doctrine / physical packages\",\"discovery_path\":\"private-search sweep → published circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Tuggle\",\"court\":\"7th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2021-07-14\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"4 F.4th 505; No. 20-2352\",\"proposition\":\"Eighteen months of pole-camera surveillance of areas exposed to public view was not a search under existing doctrine, though the opinion warned that pervasive, networked surveillance could eventually require a different constitutional answer.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Katz / pole cameras / mosaic theory\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024-2025 pole-camera opinions → repeatedly cited pre-2024 foundation\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Leaders of a Beautiful Struggle v. Baltimore Police Department\",\"court\":\"4th Cir. en banc\",\"date\":\"2021-06-24\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"2 F.4th 330\",\"proposition\":\"Baltimore's aerial-surveillance program effected a search by enabling retrospective reconstruction of individuals' movements over time; the en banc court applied Carpenter's whole-of-movements principle beyond CSLI.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Katz / aggregated public movements / mosaic theory\",\"discovery_path\":\"Chatrie and pole-camera opinions → pre-2024 doctrine-shaping authority\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Moore-Bush\",\"court\":\"1st Cir. en banc\",\"date\":\"2022-06-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"36 F.4th 320; Nos. 19-1582, 19-1626, 19-1625, 19-1583\",\"proposition\":\"The en banc court fractured evenly over whether eight months of pole-camera surveillance was a search and affirmed on good-faith grounds, making the competing public-view and mosaic approaches central to later circuit analysis.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Katz / pole cameras / fractured mosaic analysis\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024-2025 pole-camera opinions → pre-2024 en banc foundation\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Packard v. Budaj\",\"court\":\"10th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2023-11-14\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"86 F.4th 859; No. 22-1365\",\"proposition\":\"Applying Torres, intentionally striking protesters with beanbag or foam-baton rounds to exercise physical control constitutes a seizure even without arrest or successful submission; the decision became the Tenth Circuit's principal modern projectile-seizure precedent.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Torres line / less-lethal projectiles\",\"discovery_path\":\"Epps citation chain → pre-2024 doctrine-shaping authority\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Dundon v. Kirchmeier\",\"court\":\"8th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2023-11-03\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"85 F.4th 1250; No. 22-1246\",\"proposition\":\"The court held that it was not clearly established that force used for the purpose of dispersing a crowd constituted a seizure, leaving the merits unresolved but establishing the dispersal-versus-restraint fault line later applied in Marks and Keup.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Torres line / crowd dispersal / qualified immunity\",\"discovery_path\":\"Marks and Keup citation chain → pre-2024 doctrine-shaping authority\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"}}
